cat > ../../HYPOTHESIS.md <<'EOF'
# Lagrangian endpoint pricing with best-of-k measured moves and ratcheting WNS floor

## Problem
Post-stock `runTnsRecovery` ranked drivers by a memoryless sum of endpoint violations, accepted the first improving move, and used a fixed stock-WNS floor. It saturates on shared trunks (Ibex fanout-943), misses independent cones (JPEG), and gives back WNS.

## Current heuristic
`src/policy/SetupLegacyPolicy.cc: SetupLegacyPolicy::runTnsRecovery` (stock loop `runMainRepairLoop` unchanged).

## New approach
- Each violating endpoint has a price lambda_e (init 1; x(1+0.5*slack/WNS) per round while violated, x0.5 decay once repaired). Drivers are ranked by sum of lambda_e over distinct downstream endpoints (worst-path expansion of up to 400 endpoints).
- Per driver, best-of-3: measure up to 3 distinct move types from the same journal checkpoint (each rolled back), pick the best TNS, re-apply that type only, re-verify, commit.
- Commit requires WNS >= ratcheting floor max(stock-final, best-so-far) and a veto: none of the 16 highest-price endpoints may lose >0.2 ps slack.
- Failed drivers are skipped in later rounds (until something commits), so repricing reaches deeper drivers. Budget: 6 rounds / 600 trials.

## Why WNS/TNS should improve
Prices persistently concentrate effort on endpoints that stay violated (independent JPEG cones), best-of-k avoids first-improving local traps, ratchet + veto prevent WNS give-back.

## Path report
Fewer residual violators in deep cones; WNS never below stock; buffers/sizes on drivers with high price. Check `RSZ_EVOLVE lr_*` counters, especially lr_rank_changed_vs_static.

## Risks
Runtime from best-of-k trials (capped at 600); gains may not survive detailed placement; veto may block AES moves.

## Files changed
- src/policy/SetupLegacyPolicy.cc: rewrote runTnsRecovery (LR pricing, best-of-k, ratchet, veto, counters).
EOF
